  "summary_text": "Kyle, you're up 12% month-to-date at Port Washington and running 8.3% ahead of DOW-adjusted expectation\u2014real performance gain, not calendar luck. Hicksville is also beating adjusted forecast at 12.9%, with grooming revenue nearly doubling year-over-year. You should be proud of the appointment velocity across both stores: Port up 17% and Hicksville up 41% week-to-date. This is a demand signal worth protecting.\n\nHowever, there's a structural problem hiding in the numbers. Port's add-on attach rate dropped from 50% to 47% and Hicksville's cratered from 54% to 18%\u2014that's not noise, that's a discipline break. At Port, you're also running $8 per groomer below trailing-30-day baseline ($443 vs $483), which means the team is below peak productivity despite strong volume. Hicksville's groomer productivity is actually up, but the add-on collapse suggests front-of-house is not upselling as aggressively when traffic spikes.\n\nRetail is a secondary concern but worth flagging: Hicksville's retail revenue dropped 44% despite a 29% transaction increase, implying lower attach or missed opportunities. Port's retail is strong, so this is a Hicksville execution issue, not a demand issue. Both stores have inventory holes\u2014X-Small Treat Tables are in negative stock at both locations\u2014fix that this week.\n\nCapacity note: You have 7 groomers today and 4 tomorrow at Port (7 and 8 bather hours respectively), and 4 today / 3 tomorrow at Hicksville (zero bather support both days); over the next 7 days you're scheduled for 56 groomer-days combined with only 43 bather hours total, which will constrain throughput on groom-heavy days."
